{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_18-00-28-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nu={\n \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Read the retained receipt of the latest successor (CheckRun 7401ab5a8f7c4cd69d947e972ea31a61, exit 0, 28 events). Both verification cases report success without a voter quorum (events 23 and 26) and the added bound phase now measures the exposure: the isolated leader stopped reporting itself as leader after 1012 ms with a 1000 ms lease (event 24) and after 60 ms with the repository's default 50 ms lease (event 27). The account therefore states both the defect and its measured bound, and the behavior entry records the bound with its operands.\\\",\n \\\"knowledge\\\":[\n  {\\\"id\\\":\\\"97b6ea8503a34f9eacf83b6834071cc1-knowledge-0\\\",\n   \\\"kind\\\":\\\"behavior\\\",\n   \\\"statement\\\":\\\"Leader verification counts acknowledgements from non-voters: with the voter quorum unreachable and one non-voter reachable, VerifyLeader returned success with no error while the node remained leader, both with slowed timers and with the repository's default in-memory timers, because verifyLeader registers its future with every entry of the replication map and that map is built for every server in the configuration, including non-voters. The incorrect result is bounded by the lease: the same node stopped reporting itself as leader about one lease interval later (60 ms with the default 50 ms lease, 1012 ms with a 1000 ms lease).\\\",\n   \\\"conditions\\\":\\\"A configuration with more voters than the reachable set and at least one reachable non-voter; the leader can reach the non-voter but not a quorum of voters; the verification is issued before the leader lease steps the leader down, and the bound is observed while that isolation continues.\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"s-verifyleader\\\",\\\"s-replstate\\\",\\\"s-heartbeat\\\",\\\"s-lease\\\"],\n   \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"5e092a2b160b4a2f955d48ee6fa06ad9\\\":[23],\\\"c0f7723f05d8430aaf36bf2b4f9ce862\\\":[23,25],\\\"7401ab5a8f7c4cd69d947e972ea31a61\\\":[23,26,27]},\n   \\\"changed_premise\\\":\\\"Earlier revisions stated the behaviour without a measured bound, leaving the counterevidence that the lease closes the window as a source-derived claim; the latest run measures the step-down in both timer configurations, so the statement now carries the bound with its lease timeout and elapsed wait.\\\"}\n ],\n \\\"interpretations\\\":[\n  {\\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"790eb5d64ed94ef5aea4736e6f66381d-question-0\\\",\n   \\\"experiment_id\\\":\\\"f6dbda4bb1ee4baca7e8f055f109412f-experiment\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"s-verifyleader\\\",\\\"s-replstate\\\",\\\"s-heartbeat\\\",\\\"s-lease\\\",\\\"s-quorum\\\"],\n   \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"7401ab5a8f7c4cd69d947e972ea31a61\\\":[22,23,24,25,26,27],\\\"c0f7723f05d8430aaf36bf2b4f9ce862\\\":[22,23,24,25],\\\"5e092a2b160b4a2f955d48ee6fa06ad9\\\":[22,23]},\n   \\\"joint_history\\\":\\\"Three receipts answer this question. The first (5e092a2b160b4a2f955d48ee6fa06ad9 at indices 22 and 23) measured the isolation with slowed timers; the second (c0f7723f05d8430aaf36bf2b4f9ce862 at 22-25) ran it with slowed and default timers; the third (7401ab5a8f7c4cd69d947e972ea31a61 at 22-27) repeats both cases and adds the bounded wait that measures when the isolated leader stops reporting itself as leader.\\\",\n   \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Current account: leader verification can be satisfied without a voter quorum, and the resulting incorrect answer is available for about one lease interval before the same node stops leading. Evidence: in the latest receipt the slowed-timer setup event 22 records three voters with a quorum of two, a voter unreachable by direct AppendEntries and a reachable non-voter, and its result event 23 records verification succeeding with no error in under a millisecond while the node still led; events 25 and 26 repeat the premise and the same result with the repository's default in-memory timers; the bound events 24 and 27 record the node becoming a follower after 1012 ms with a 1000 ms lease and after 60 ms with the default 50 ms lease. The earlier receipts show the same two verification results, so the finding is reproduced across three runs. Mechanism from source: verifyLeader starts its count at one for itself and registers the future in every entry of the leader's replication map, which startStopReplication builds for every server of the configuration except the leader without filtering by suffrage; each peer's heartbeat acknowledges the pending verification on a successful AppendEntries, so a non-voter's acknowledgement raises the count to the voter quorum computed from voters alone, while the lease check counts voters only and therefore closes the window about one lease interval later.\\\",\n   \\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"violated\\\",\n   \\\"defect_claim\\\":\\\"asserted\\\",\n   \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\n    \\\"The measured bound limits the impact: the node stops reporting itself as leader about one lease interval after losing the voter quorum, so the incorrect verification result is available only inside that interval.\\\",\n    \\\"The defect requires a configuration containing non-voters; in a voters-only cluster every replication peer is a voter and the acknowledgement set equals the voter set, so the path cannot diverge from the voter quorum.\\\",\n    \\\"No write path is affected: entry commitment uses the commitment map, which contains voters only, so this is a read-verification path rather than a commit-rule path.\\\",\n    \\\"The probe calls VerifyLeader directly rather than through an application that then serves a read, so the stale-read consequence is inferred from the documented purpose of the API rather than measured end to end.\\\"\n   ],\n   \\\"limitations\\\":[\n    \\\"Measured on four-peer in-memory clusters with the repository's own facilities; production timings and transports are not covered.\\\",\n    \\\"The bound is reported for the two timer configurations used (1000 ms and the default 50 ms lease) and depends on the lease timeout, not on a fixed wall-clock constant.\\\",\n    \\\"Both cases isolate the leader by transport disconnection and confirm it with direct AppendEntries attempts recorded in the same events.\\\"\n   ]}\n ]\n}\njson.dump(u,open('update.json','w'),indent=2); print('written')\nPY\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_18-00-28-hashicorp_raft-real-run --submission update.json 2>&1 | tail -2\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
